Why GPL-2.0-or-later, and not a permissive license
--------------------------------------------------

This package is a derivative work of RELION, which is GPL-2.0-or-later
(each source file in RELION says "version 2 of the License, or (at your
option) any later version"). Specifically:

  * backend/relion_webui/generated_schema.py, generated_commands.py and
    generated_cli_options.py are extracted mechanically from RELION's
    C++ sources (pipeline_jobs.cpp, gui_jobwindow.cpp and RELION's Typer
    programs). generated_schema.py carries RELION's option help text
    essentially verbatim: 330 of 335 strings match the C++ sources.
  * backend/relion_webui/job_commands.py ports the getCommands* functions
    one by one, citing the source line for each.
  * backend/relion_webui/job_specs.py transcribes RELION's output-node tables.
  * backend/relion_webui/_vendor.py reimplements RELION's filtered tilt-series
    writer to produce the same on-disk layout.

A permissive license (MIT, BSD, Apache) therefore cannot be used: the
code embodies GPL-2.0-or-later material. "or-later" is required rather
than "GPL-2.0-only" because this package depends on Apache-2.0 licensed
libraries (python-multipart, and the OpenAI/Anthropic/Google SDKs used by
the optional assistant), which are compatible with GPL-3.0 but not with
GPL-2.0-only. Taking the "or later" grant is what makes those
dependencies compatible.

RELION itself: https://github.com/3dem/relion
